] by claiming T2 from discard? But I also have T2 in hand now?\n\nWait \u2014 conflict.\n\nHand: [W1, W4, W5, W6, W8, T2, T3, B7]\n\nBut meld: [T1, T2, T3] \u2014 that\u2019s a chow.\n\nSo total T2: one in meld, one in hand \u2192 two T2s? But there are only 4 copies.\n\nFrom game history: I played T2 twice: Play T2, Play T2 \u2014 and I did Chi T2.\n\nLet\u2019s reconstruct:\n\n- I performed \"Chi T2\" \u2014 meaning I used a discarded T2 to complete [T1,T2,T3] chow. So I contributed T1 and T3 from hand, took T2.\n- Then later, I played T2 twice.\n\nThat implies I had at least two T2s in hand after forming the chow? Impossible \u2014 you can't have a tile both in meld and hand unless duplicated.\n\nUnless... no: when you chi, you place the three tiles down. You cannot have another T2 in hand unless you drew it later.\n\nBut total T2 available: 4 copies.\n\nUnshown: T2: 1 \u2192 so 3 have been discarded or used.\n\nMy history: I played T2 twice \u2192 so I discarded two T2s.\n\nAnd I used one T2 in chow \u2192 total 3 T2s accounted for by me.\n\nBut unshown says T2: 1 \u2192 so one still in wall or unused.\n\nYes, possible: I had T2 in hand, used one to form chow (no \u2014 when you chi, you take the discard, so you don\u2019t need to have the claimed tile).\n\nStandard rule: When performing 'Chi', you use two tiles from your hand and claim the discarded tile to complete the sequence.\n\nSo for \"Chi T2\" to form [T1,T2,T3], I must have had T1 and T3 in hand, and claimed the discarded T2.\n\nThen, later, I discarded T2 twice \u2014 meaning I drew additional T2s afterward.\n\nTotal T2s I used: one in meld (the claimed one), and two discarded \u2014 total 3.\n\nUnshown: T2: 1 \u2192 yes, one remains.\n\nSo I currently have T2 in hand \u2014 that\u2019s the fourth copy.\n\nSimilarly, T3: I had one in hand (used for chi), and now another T3 in hand \u2192 second T3.\n\nPossible.\n\nSo current hand: W1, W4, W5, W6, W8, T2, T3, B7\n\nMelds: [J1,J1,J1], [T1,T2,T3]\n\nNow, back to evaluation.\n\nHaving T2 and T3 again \u2014 could I form another [T2,T3,T4]? But T4 is unshown: 2 left, and I don\u2019t have T4.\n\nB7: isolated.\n\nW4-W5-W6: consecutive \u2014 very promising. Could form a chow.\n\nW8: could go for W7-W8-W9, but W7 has only 1 left, and opponent has W7 in meld \u2014 risky.\n\nBest move: keep W4,W5,W6 together for a strong chow.\n\nDiscard **W1** \u2014 it's the most useless tile.\n\nAlternatively, **B7** \u2014 but B7 could be part of future chow, though no support.\n\nOr **T3** \u2014 but I have T2 and T3, could wait for T4.\n\nBut T4 has 2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nHowever, forming another bamboo chow may not help much.\n\nBetter to focus on **Characters** \u2014 I have W4,W5,W6 \u2014 a near-complete chow.\n\nAlso, W5 and W6 are central, good for **Closed Wait**.\n\nIf I can complete W4-W5-W6, and draw W6 to pair? No, I have only one W6.\n\nWait \u2014 hand has W6:1 \u2192 one copy.\n\nSo if I keep W4,W5,W6, I can form the chow [W4,W5,W6].\n\nThen I need two more melds or a pair.\n\nRemaining tiles: W1, W8, T2, T3, B7\n\nStill messy.\n\nPerhaps best to aim for **Chicken Hand** with at least:\n- Dragon Pung (2)\n- One more pung? Unlikely.\n- Or go for **Half Flush**? But I have multiple suits.\n\nAnother idea: **All Types (6 pts)** \u2014 requires all three suits, winds, dragons.\n\nI have:\n- Characters (W)\n- Bamboos (T)\n- Dots? B7 \u2014 yes, B7 is Dots\n- Dragons: J1 \u2014 yes\n- Winds: F2 is South, but I don\u2019t have any wind tile\n\nSo if I could get a wind pung, but I don\u2019t.\n\nNot feasible.\n\nBest immediate move: **Play W1**\n\nBecause:\n- It\u2019s a terminal tile, not contributing to middle sequences.\n- Holding it reduces flexibility.\n- W4-W5-W6 is a better core.\n\nOther options:\n- **Play B7**: B7 is somewhat live, but no support.\n- **Play T3**: might break potential T2-T3-T4, but T4 is still available.\n- But T2 and T3 are simples, could be useful.\n\nSince I already have a chow in Bamboo, adding another might help **Pure Double Chow** (1 pt) if same numbers, but unlikely.\n\n**Reason**: Discarding W1 minimizes loss of useful tiles while removing a terminal that hinders simpler patterns. It keeps the promising W4-W5-W6 sequence intact, potentially leading to **Closed Wait (2 pts)** if I draw W5 or W6 to complete a chow, and supports **Dragon Pung (2 pts)** and possibly **Outside Hand (4 pts)** if the pair is a terminal/honor.\n\nBut to maximize safety and maintain value, **Play W1** is optimal.\n\nAnswer: Play W1\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid actions, discarding W1 removes a terminal tile that has limited utility in forming valuable patterns, while preserving the strong W4-W5-W6 sequence which can develop into a chow with potential for Closed Wait. Other discards like B7 or T3 could disrupt possible future chows. W1 offers no such benefit and increases the risk of being stuck with a terminal pair.\n\nReason: W1 is a dead-end tile with no support in hand, whereas keeping W4-W5-W6 provides a solid foundation for a middle-based chow. This improves chances of achieving Closed Wait (2 pts), and combined with existing Dragon Pung (2 pts), Outside Hand (4 pts) becomes possible if the final pair is a terminal or honor.\n\nAnswer: Play W1"}
